for this week's mini election trip I'm  off to Oxford West and Abingdon to visit  the Conservative MP Nicola Blackwood and  find out how her campaign to hold one of  the most marginal seat in the country is  good how do you say your campaigns going  so far are you in full-on electioneering  mode or is this just your day to day  your day to day canvassing well we've  been in full-on election mode for quite  some time and but we've been canvassing  three times a week and twice on  Saturdays for many months now this is  not just so that we can have the right  information for Election Day so and it's  also so that I can get right information  in order to be able to do my job as an  MP I think you saw that today what we're  doing a survey canvassing we're not only  asking people about their views about  voting but we're also asking people  about their views about the things which  I should be working on and how I can  better represent them so is a  combination of the two really and what  kind of messages have you been hearing  on the doorstep from people well we're  hearing people who say that they are  strongly back and conservatives you have  one lady and they want to hear strong  messages coming out and lift generating  messages well they want to hear the  Prime Minister speaking out and being  clear that the Conservatives are doing a  good job in government which I was very  happy to endorse and we're also hearing  people who are talking about units some  of the infrastructure ones we have  locally they want a strong community  champion in their MP and that's what I'm  trying to do for that this is one of the  most marginal conservative seats in the  country with no point three percent I  believe is your majority there's a good  chance the Lib Dem vote is not going to  be as strong and does that make you feel  confident in the Wales it's still a  battle when you go on doorsteps with Lib  Dem voters well having a majority of 176  does not make you confident it makes you  feel like you have to fight for every  vote and I feel like as an MP that's a  good attitude to have anyway I don't  think that we should have a an ounce of  complacency um clearly we you know we  look at the polling and it gives us you  know some confidence that we're doing  some good things here and that people  know what we're doing um but when it  comes down to it we're going to fight  for every last vote because  is a lot can happen between now and Paul  day and we can't take anything wrong  since you've entered Parliament there's  one of the 2010 as the big intake of 12  of young Conservative MPs and you've  certainly been less vocal and in the  national media then some of your other  your other compadres it was that a  decision because you're such a marginal  seat and was it something it just  happened naturally well I sort of made a  conscious decision that I wanted to  focus very much on fighting for my  constituency I wanted to get to the 8th  of May and know that I had done  absolutely everything I could to fight  for this seat that I wouldn't have any  regrets that I hadn't focused enough on  it I learnt a very big lesson when my  opponent at the last election turned  down a that was everlasting it was  turned out there very important hustings  to go and do an interview in London and  sent his agent instead I can tell you  that for 300 businesses in the room that  went down like a lead balloon I never  wanted to be that MP so I focused very  hard on being a good constituency MP and  doing my work in Parliament to the best  of my ability and we'll have to see what  kind of response occurs that's it for  our first stop of the mini election to  join me next week to find out how the  general election is being fought in a  different part of the country
 

